WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:09.448
This diagram shows how AWS Resource Access Manager shares supported resources without transferring ownership.

2
00:00:09.448 --> 00:00:11.716
Begin in Account A on the left.

3
00:00:11.716 --> 00:00:19.461
It owns a Transit Gateway or a subnet and remains responsible for configuration, lifecycle and operational management.

4
00:00:19.461 --> 00:00:22.954
Follow the existing arrow into the RAM resource share.

5
00:00:22.954 --> 00:00:29.268
The owner selects the resource, the consumers, and the managed permissions governing allowed actions.

6
00:00:29.268 --> 00:00:33.791
On the upper right, Accounts B and C are in the same organization.

7
00:00:33.791 --> 00:00:40.080
They gain permitted use, never ownership; the diagram assumes organization sharing is enabled.

8
00:00:40.234 --> 00:00:43.199
The dashed lower branch is for an external account.

9
00:00:43.199 --> 00:00:48.663
Invitation acceptance and resource-type support for external sharing are required.

10
00:00:48.663 --> 00:00:55.828
In the subnet example below, the VPC owner controls the VPC, subnets and route lifecycle.

11
00:00:55.828 --> 00:01:03.393
The participant can launch and manage its own approved resources in the shared subnet, but does not gain VPC administration.

12
00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:08.470
The lower-left note separates RAM share permission from the participant’s IAM permission.

13
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:09.488
Both matter.

14
00:01:09.488 --> 00:01:16.370
The remaining notes require organization sharing to be enabled and warn that billing depends on service and usage.

15
00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.496
The owner retains operations.

16
00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.416
Return to the full diagram.

17
00:01:20.416 --> 00:01:27.001
Share access intentionally, and keep resource ownership and operational responsibilities explicit.

18
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:38.547
This slide separates sharing a Transit Gateway from configuring a working network path.

19
00:01:38.547 --> 00:01:45.107
AWS RAM shares the gateway; VPC owners still attach and route their own networks.

20
00:01:45.107 --> 00:01:48.083
Start with the network account above the gateway.

21
00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:49.900
It owns the Transit Gateway.

22
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:57.594
The purple sharing rail reaches the two VPC owner accounts; sharing grants attachment access, not ready-made routes.

23
00:01:57.709 --> 00:02:03.780
On the left, App A is in the ten dot zero dot zero dot zero slash sixteen VPC.

24
00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:12.634
Its VPC route sends traffic for the ten dot one dot zero dot zero slash sixteen destination network toward the Transit Gateway.

25
00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:17.646
Follow the existing amber path through the A attachment into the Transit Gateway.

26
00:02:17.646 --> 00:02:23.059
The receiving attachment association selects the inbound Transit Gateway route table.

27
00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:26.164
Look below the gateway at the route lookup example.

28
00:02:26.164 --> 00:02:32.505
The destination ten dot one dot zero dot zero slash sixteen points to the B attachment.

29
00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:38.275
Continue along the amber path through the B attachment to App B in the right-hand VPC.

30
00:02:38.275 --> 00:02:40.427
Now follow the cyan path back.

31
00:02:40.427 --> 00:02:47.721
VPC B also needs a route for VPC A through the gateway, and the gateway needs the corresponding return route.

32
00:02:47.721 --> 00:02:50.041
A forward path alone is not enough.

33
00:02:50.041 --> 00:02:56.548
The lower-left note assigns VPC attachments and VPC routes to the VPC owners.

34
00:02:56.653 --> 00:03:02.686
The next notes assign attachment associations and route propagation to the Transit Gateway owner.

35
00:03:02.686 --> 00:03:11.449
Association selects the route table for incoming traffic; propagation advertises VPC address ranges into configured tables.

36
00:03:11.449 --> 00:03:17.351
The bottom line reminds us that security groups, network ACLs and DNS still apply.

37
00:03:17.351 --> 00:03:19.335
Return to the full architecture.

38
00:03:19.335 --> 00:03:24.696
Sharing, attachment ownership, route lookup and the return path are separate checks.
